          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.2 Ratios in Maps"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.118,
            0.846,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.154,
                0.118,
                0.846,
                0.492
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_1_image_2.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.504,
            0.846,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Have you noticed that in many maps there is a ratio given, usually in the lower right corner of the map? It usually contains 1 and a very large number, such as 1 : 60,00,000. What does RF 1 : 60,00,000 mean? What does it indicate? Can you guess?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.583,
            0.845,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A Representative Fraction (RF) is an expression that shows the ratio between a distance on the map and the corresponding actual distance on the ground."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.638,
            0.845,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For example, if the ratio on a map is 1 : 60,00,000, that means a distance of 1 cm on the map is equivalent to a geographical distance of 60,00,000 cm. Remember, this is geographical distance and not road distance!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.704,
            0.501,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Convert 60,00,000 cm to kilometres."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.729,
            0.373,
            0.749
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It is 60 km. Verify this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.758,
            0.846,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Using the map above, can you find the geographical distance between Bengaluru and Chennai? Also, find the geographical distance between Mangaluru and Chennai."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.82,
            0.846,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "["
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Use a ruler to find the distance between the cities on the map. Then, use the ratio given on the map to find the actual geographical distance.]"
            }
          ]
        },
